## Touchpoint 2 — Pre-coding analysis + LLD (after intake, BEFORE any code edit)

Salesforce is a heavily-cascading runtime: one insert can fire a process builder → flow → trigger → another trigger updating a third sObject. A "just create one record" change can ripple into 4-5 sObjects and queued jobs. Walk that landscape BEFORE coding, while the accidental ripples are still cheap to design around.

Run these as a `TodoWrite` plan (one entry each) for resumability:

- **E1 — Identify the touched surface.** List every component the change reads/writes/triggers: sObjects (confirm shapes in `config/schema/`), triggers (`grep -l "<sObject>" force-app/main/default/triggers/*.trigger`), flows, validation rules (`ls .../objects/<sObj>/validationRules/`), OmniStudio (`grep` the IP/DR/OS dirs), Apex callers, custom metadata/settings.
- **E2 — Map cascading impact.** For each create/update/delete, walk downstream: which triggers + flows fire, what DML they do, what other sObjects they touch — recurse until the chain terminates. Sketch it as a mermaid `flowchart` for the doc, following [`docs/diagram-conventions.md`](../../docs/diagram-conventions.md): **one subgraph per component**, never per logical phase, so the diagram shows blast radius and ownership rather than just the happy path. A legend line goes immediately above the fence, and colour carries state only — the defect in amber, a dead end in red.
- **E3 — Classify intended vs accidental.** Tag each downstream effect INTENDED (matches an AC) or ACCIDENTAL. **Each ACCIDENTAL effect becomes a question to confirm with the user BEFORE coding.** INTENDED effects become evidence in the doc.
- **E4 — Author the draft LLD FIRST** (see below). This is the design-first gate — do NOT edit source until the LLD's current-behavior + gap + proposed-design sections exist. Reading current source is required.
- **E5 — Pass adversarial Gate A, when the gate applies.** [`adversarial-review.mdc`](./adversarial-review.mdc) decides that; do not gate work it exempts. When it does apply, launch the two defect critics plus the minimality critic in one parallel fan-out against the same LLD revision, and record reviewer provenance, findings, dispositions, residual risk, and re-review in the LLD. Resolve every blocker before editing.
- **E6 — Spawn the preliminary `changes/<slug>.md`**, filling only its architecture/design sections as a SHORT summary that links to the LLD. Leave the rest as placeholders. **Do NOT commit the LLD or the change doc yet** — both are working files until wrap-up.

Keep E1-E4 proportionate: a typo, a label rename, or a self-contained component nothing consumes yet needs a short answer, not a ceremony. [`adversarial-review.mdc`](./adversarial-review.mdc) is the sole authority on whether E5 runs at all — it exempts work with no material risk, and the user can waive it outright. When it is exempt, say so in one line and move on; when it applies, planning-only work ends only after Gate A passes.

### The Low-Level Design (LLD)

The design output of pre-coding analysis. Copy [`docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_lld.md`](../../docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_lld.md) to `docs/lld/<work-id>-<short-kebab-slug>.md`. It proves you understand the system before touching it: what it does today, the precise gap, and the minimal proposed change with alternatives weighed and cascading impact mapped.

Sections: header + summary; Purpose & scope; Intake (transcribed AC text); Domain mapping; Current behavior (+ diagram); Gap analysis; Before-state evidence; Proposed design (+ diagram); Affected components; Cascading impact; adversarial Gate A evidence/dispositions; Draft test scenarios; Open questions; gated pre-implementation checklist; References.

The LLD carries **two** diagrams — current behavior and proposed design — drawn per [`docs/diagram-conventions.md`](../../docs/diagram-conventions.md) with the **same node IDs in both**, so the amber node in the first is the green node in the second and a reader tracks what moved by eye. Anything the change strands stays in the second diagram in grey rather than disappearing from it.

Authoring rules:
- **Read the latest source, don't guess** — pull every component you describe and cite concrete paths/methods/fields.
- **Evidence over assertion** — back the gap with read-only queries and numbers; back behavior with actual code paths.
- **Diagram by component, not by phase** — a subgraph is one deployable thing with a name you could put in a manifest. Node labels carry the diagnosis (`Name only · no sort · first record`), not just the element name, and decision edges quote the real predicate values so they stay checkable against the metadata. Full conventions in [`docs/diagram-conventions.md`](../../docs/diagram-conventions.md).
- **Design minimally** — smallest additive change reusing existing mechanisms; always list rejected alternatives so the next person doesn't re-litigate.
- **Separate decided from pending** — park unconfirmed assumptions in the Open-questions section; never bake an unconfirmed predicate into the design as settled.

The LLD is mandatory for non-trivial work. Two **conditional** companions (do NOT produce by reflex): `docs/lld/<work-id>-questions-and-kt.md` (only when assumptions need stakeholder confirmation; [`_TEMPLATE_questions-and-kt.md`](../../docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_questions-and-kt.md)) and `docs/lld/<work-id>-walkthrough.md` (only when a live alignment session is warranted; [`_TEMPLATE_walkthrough.md`](../../docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_walkthrough.md)). The change doc summarizes + links to the LLD — never duplicate the full design into both.

