## Touchpoint 3 — Implementation gate + wrap-up (`changes/` doc + two-commit)

When implementation and validation tests are ready, run adversarial Gate B — if the gate applies — **before** the first real deploy, org DML, cache-bust/version swap, commit, or final handoff. Supply the evidence pack (base SHA, explicit changed-path list, `git diff --stat`) plus profile-relevant validation output. Any change to the artifact or its evidence starts a new revision and reruns all three critics together, with any accepted minimality finding passed forward to the two defect critics.

**The change doc does not get its own gate.** It records work the gates already reviewed, so reviewing the record adds cost without adding coverage. Finalize it after deploy and the code commit, then commit it. Ask for an agent-guidance review only if you specifically want one.

### Pick a template & name the doc

Copy from `changes/_templates/` to `changes/<short-kebab-slug>.md`:
- `_TEMPLATE_bugfix.md` — defect/regression/incident (heavy on root cause + symptom timeline).
- `_TEMPLATE_story.md` — new functionality (heavy on requirements + AC verification).
- `_TEMPLATE_refactor.md` — internal restructuring, no user-visible change (heavy on behavioral-invariant parity).
- `_TEMPLATE_retrieve.md` — after an org-wide mirror retrieve; saves to `changes/git/retrieve-<date>-<time>-<alias>.md` and follows [`docs/org-mirror/README.md`](../../docs/org-mirror/README.md) (plan-first: spawn the full phase plan as todos before any `sf` retrieve). A retrieve run is `change_kind: retrieve_mirror` — **Gate B only**, on the finished audit doc.

Slug = kebab-case description of the work, NOT the ticket number (`account-trigger-recursion-fix.md`, not `STORY-12345.md`). The ticket reference lives inside the doc.

### Header block (every doc)

```markdown
**Date:** YYYY-MM-DD
**Sandbox:** `{{ORG_ALIAS}}`
**Lead:** <Name> (<role>)
**Story / ticket:** [<TRACKER-NNN>](<url>) — <one-line summary>
**Code commit(s):** [`<short-hash>`](#deploy-ids-and-commit-references) (latest; full list in section X)
**Manifest:** [`manifest/<feature>.xml`](../manifest/<feature>.xml) (deploy manifest; XML inlined in section X)
**Step-by-step test evidence:**
- `<test-org-alias>` — [`docs/ut/<work-id>/<slug>-<alias-slug>-step-by-step-test.md`](../docs/ut/<work-id>/<slug>-<alias-slug>-step-by-step-test.md) · initial `<hash / pending>` · verified `<hash / pending tester>`
- `<additional-org-alias>` — `<guide + initial/verified hashes, repeat as needed>` / n/a
**Status:** Resolved / Delivered / In progress / Pending observation
```

`**Manifest:**` is REQUIRED whenever the work used `--manifest`; inline the full XML in the Deploy-IDs section so reviewers don't open another file.

### Two-commit strategy (mandatory): code first, doc second

Prerequisite, where Gate B applied: it is `PASS` or `PASS_WITH_FINDINGS`, with no Critical/High and every Medium resolved or explicitly accepted by the user. Where the gate did not apply or the user waived it, the one-line record of that is the prerequisite instead.

Immediately before the code commit, re-run `git diff --name-only <base>` and confirm the changed-path list still matches what Gate B reviewed — if a path appeared or vanished, that is a new revision and the gate reruns. Before the doc commit, the doc must be final: deploy IDs, log IDs, commit hashes, and all prose already filled in.

1. **Stage ONLY the files you intentionally modified** — name every path explicitly. NEVER blanket-add a directory (`git add force-app/`) — another agent/user/job may have in-flight changes you'd mangle. Sanity-check with `git status -s`; if you lost track, recover with `git diff --name-only [--cached|HEAD]`. If a dirty file isn't yours, leave it — never silently revert it. (Exception: the org-wide retrieve workflow commits the whole mirrored `force-app/` as one snapshot by design.)
   If unrelated dirty changes prevent a complete Gate B snapshot, use an isolated worktree or stop and coordinate with the owner; do not omit changed paths and claim a complete review.
2. **Commit the code** with a multi-line HEREDOC message (subject ≤72 chars imperative; what/why; sub-changes).
3. **Capture the hash:** `git log -1 --format='%H%n%h%n%s'` — the short hash goes in the header + Deploy-IDs section.
4. **Finalize the doc**, fill in the Gate A/B outcome sections, then commit it SEPARATELY. Never inline the doc into the code commit. The doc itself is not gated — see above.
5. **Verify:** `git log -2` + `git status -s`; report both hashes.

### Filling the doc

- Header: paste the confirmed ticket from intake; fill `**Manifest:**` if applicable.
- Header: add one step-by-step evidence row per tested alias with its guide and initial/verified hashes; otherwise state why the block is n/a.
- Requirements: paste the transcribed AC text verbatim (one row per AC, Source column = origin); out-of-scope as a bullet list. NEVER "see attached screenshot".
- Revision log (near the top): first row = date, hash, what, why.
- Adversarial-review section: record Gate B status, reviewed diff/revision, reviewer identities/lenses, category coverage, findings, dispositions, accepted risks, tests added, and re-review verdicts. Link Gate A design findings from the LLD rather than duplicating them.
- **Key changes — diff highlights:** for OmniScripts / IPs / DataRaptors / FlexiPages / layouts / validation rules / formula fields / new Apex classes the raw `git diff` is unreadable — paste a trimmed ```diff``` snippet (small/medium changes) OR cite line ranges + key method names (large/new files). Skip only for 1-2 line changes where the diff is self-evident.
- **Acceptance-criteria visual delta:** for visually observable ACs, add paired, component-scoped before/current Mermaid excerpts and link each one to the matching test-guide anchor. Link rather than duplicate the detailed procedure. Data-only ACs keep query/log evidence and state `n/a — no visual delta`.
- Replace every `<...>` placeholder and delete the guidance comments; write `n/a` (with a reason) for inapplicable sections. Keep the **Gate A / Gate B outcome** sections themselves — they are the audit trail for the review, not guidance.

### Same thread, same doc

When a thread iterates on the SAME flow/class/OmniScript, use ONE doc — don't spawn a new one per iteration. Append the new commit hash to the header, add a Revision-log row, add a Diff-highlights sub-section, and restate §1 (TL;DR) to the CUMULATIVE state. If iteration N+1 invalidates N, restate §1/§7 to the latest stable state and mark the superseded Revision-log row `(superseded by <hash>)` — never delete it. Start a NEW doc only when the work shifts to a clearly different feature, or the previous doc merged to `main`.

